---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Banner from '../components/Banner.astro';
import Logo from '../components/Logo.astro';
import { aboutImage } from '../config/images';
import { siteData } from '../data/siteData';
---

<BaseLayout title="About" description="Why PickleMyPaddle exists: keeping good pickleball paddles in the game for longer.">
  <Banner title="About PickleMyPaddle" intro="Why we pickle paddles, and who does the work." />

  <section class="py-section">
    <div class="mx-auto grid max-w-site grid-cols-1 items-center gap-10 px-4 sm:px-6 lg:grid-cols-2 lg:gap-14">
      <div class="flex aspect-4/3 items-center justify-center overflow-hidden rounded-lg bg-tint-green">
        {aboutImage ? (
          <Image src={aboutImage} alt="The person behind PickleMyPaddle" width={800} height={600} class="size-full object-cover" />
        ) : (
          <span class="placeholder">[To confirm: photo of you at a court or the workbench]</span>
        )}
      </div>
      <div class="space-y-5 leading-relaxed text-text-muted">
        <h2 class="font-heading text-h2 font-bold text-text">Pickled, not retired.</h2>
        <p>
          The name is a play on words. To pickle something is to preserve it, and that's what we do for paddles. A
          good paddle doesn't need retiring just because its face has worn smooth. Re-gritting restores the surface
          texture that grips the ball, so a paddle you already know and trust stays in play for longer.
        </p>
        <p>
          It's better for your game, kinder to your wallet than a new paddle, and keeps one more paddle out of landfill.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="pb-section">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl space-y-5 px-4 leading-relaxed text-text-muted sm:px-6">
      <h2 class="font-heading text-h2 font-bold text-text">Who we are</h2>
      <p>
        <strong class="text-text">I’m an engineer who fell into the world of pickleball in 2026.</strong>
        Before that, I spent over four decades playing tennis—and the last three decades restringing my own racquets.
        When I went to buy my first pickleball paddle, I was blown away by how expensive they are. Compared to a
        tennis racquet, which I can quickly re-string whenever needed, it felt crazy to throw a paddle away just
        because the face wore down.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you consider re-stringing a tennis racquet functionally equivalent to re-gritting a pickleball paddle, it
        stands to reason we should be able to restore them, right?
      </p>
      <p>
        Now, a quick disclaimer: pickleball officials haven’t listed re-gritting as a permitted alteration. For now,
        if you re-grit your paddle, you can only use it for social and practice play. A refurbished paddle will almost
        certainly not be permitted in official tournaments.
      </p>

      <h3 class="pt-4 font-heading text-h3 font-bold text-text">Rescuing your trusted paddle from landfill</h3>
      <p>
        Anyway, legalities aside, you likely have a paddle you paid good money for. There’s nothing wrong with it
        structurally; it’s just lost its bite on the ball. Being a problem solver by nature, I figured my fellow
        pickleballers would love a way to put the spin back into their favourite paddle instead of buying a new one.
      </p>
      <p>
        I looked at what others were doing online. I saw a few YouTube videos where people DIY’d a fix using
        hardware-store Gorilla Glue and skateboard targeted gritting solution. While it technically works, even the
        finest skateboard grit is way too coarse. It gives the paddle too much bite, completely changing how it plays
        compared to a factory-standard paddle.
      </p>
      {/*
        REVIEW (SCRUM-30), early Nov 2026: the ~20 g figure is an estimate. Replace it with measured grams once the
        application methods have been evaluated, and keep the matching FAQ answer in src/data/faq.ts in line.
      */}
      <p>
        So, I engineered a better way. I found a process to re-grit a paddle to get it as close to "factory legal"
        specs as possible. It restores the surface friction to maximum allowed levels while keeping the additional
        weight of the bonding agent and grit as low as I can. The process adds about 20 grams across both faces,
        adding slightly more weight than what a 50c coin weighs to the paddle.
      </p>

      <h3 class="pt-4 font-heading text-h3 font-bold text-text">A premium service for fellow players</h3>
      <p>
        It typically costs between $50 and $70 to restring a tennis racquet, and it’s a pretty straightforward job.
        Re-gritting a paddle is a bit more of an art form. It requires applying an incredibly thin, perfectly even
        layer of bonding agent and distributing the grit uniformly to get that "good as new" feel.
      </p>
      <p>
        I’ve streamlined the process to keep the costs down so you can give your favourite paddle a second lease on
        life. Use your refurbished paddle to crush it in social play and drills, and save the grit on your expensive
        tournament paddle for competition day!
      </p>

      <p class="pt-4">Based in {siteData.contact.area}, serving players by post across Australia and clubs locally.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="bg-tint-blue py-section">
    <div class="mx-auto flex max-w-site flex-col items-center gap-6 px-4 text-center sm:px-6">
      <Logo variant="full" class="h-40 w-auto" title="PickleMyPaddle logo" />
      <p class="max-w-xl text-lead">Ready to give your paddle a second life?</p>
      <a href={siteData.orderCta.href} class="btn-primary">{siteData.orderCta.label}</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
